WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.470
- There's a robot company up on a hill

00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.950
in this small coastal town
in the south-west of England.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.340
They got in touch with me and asked

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.230
if wanted to come and
look at their robots.

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:10.060
And I said, "Oh yeah, I've heard of you,

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.550
"you make really impressive robots,

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:15.240
"but they're not really that much of
a story for my channel on their own."

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.890
So I said, "look, one of the things you do

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.700
"is you make robot doubles of people

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.000
"for shows and attractions
and publicity stunts.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.235
"Could you make a robot double of me?"

00:00:23.235 --> 00:00:25.220
And they said they could...

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.680
but it would cost quite a bit of money.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:29.740
- We create humanoid robots

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.520
such as the ones behind me.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.400
Entertainment, communication and education

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.860
are the main use cases
for this type of robot.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.330
We've been creating
robots for over 15 years,

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:45.110
and in that time the technology
has obviously progressed.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:48.240
Robothespian was our first humanoid robot,

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:50.370
and that was very plasticy.

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.320
After Robothespian, we
started to create Mesmer.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.920
Mesmer is our
ultra-lifelike-looking robot.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:01.900
So it has the skin, it's painted
like a human, we have hair,

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.530
and then we also dress them like a human,

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.060
and they're of the human form.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.600
And then when we created
Ameca, our newest robot,

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.290
that's when we thought:
we need more facial expressions.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:16.350
So we increased the number
of motors in the head.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.760
There's more brow movements,
it's got inner and outer brow,

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.220
we've got a nose wrinkle,

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.760
and we've got better movement in the lips.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.380
The problem we have is the uncanny valley.

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.280
You get that uncanny resemblance,
but it's not quite right.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.410
And again, it's not just
the what-it-looks-like,

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.200
it's also how it moves.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.550
And that's where it gets creepy,

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.270
that's where it gets scary.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.580
We can create any person in robot form.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:47.960
So we can create a
Mesmer of you or anyone.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.470
- Okay, so I want them to
build the robot double,

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:52.910
not as a permanent thing,
but it'd be great as a one-off,

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.060
like, that's a great video idea.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.150
But what would I do with it?

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:57.870
And besides, even just
the parts and labour

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.100
for making the big mask
and attaching it to

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.108
an existing bot for
one day's filming...

00:02:02.108 --> 00:02:04.033
that's not cheap.

00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.380
So back in 2019, I did a video

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.180
about the advertising for
VPNs, virtual private networks.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.890
If you watch much YouTube,
you will probably have seen

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:12.950
an advert for VPNs at some point.

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:15.900
And in 2019, some of those adverts were...

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:17.750
well, "lying" is a strong term,

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:19.070
but certainly stretching the truth a bit,

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.560
and I made a video about that.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.910
Turns out that video worked
better than I thought,

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:24.610
no one's making those claims anymore.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:25.530
I have heard rumours

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.520
that there were several
quite awkward conversations

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.220
between VPN companies and the
people advertising for them.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:34.320
That video changed the industry,
it changed how VPNs were sold.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.770
And it also cut me off from
being able to advertise them.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:38.960
Partly because companies were wary of me,

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.520
partly because a lot of people
didn't quite pay attention to the video.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.230
I wasn't saying VPNs were bad,

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.270
just that VPN advertising was bad.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:47.220
There are valid reasons to use VPNs,

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.220
I said so in the video.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:49.690
"Those are valid uses of VPNs."

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:53.540
But I burned that bridge,
I couldn't advertise VPNs.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.570
You've probably already worked out
where this is going by now?

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:58.850
Inside the robot factory is my double,

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:00.740
and it's going to do
the sponsor read for me.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:01.610
I haven't met it yet.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:03.760
A few months ago, I was here
to get my head 3D-scanned,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.430
but that's all I know.

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.500
I haven't even seen a picture
of the final result yet.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.610
I'm just going to take this
GoPro camera in on a stick,

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:13.140
and meet myself.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:15.470
- So to create the Tom Scott bot,

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:19.840
we actually got Tom Scott
to sit in a scanning rig,

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.350
took a scan of Tom,

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:23.350
and then we 3D sculpt that.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:25.520
So first we clean up the scan,

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:29.010
3D sculpt the actual person
that we are creating.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.470
And then when we've got that,

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:33.270
we have to design the
mechanics that fit inside.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.820
We have a 3D printed skull,
over the top of the mechanics

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:38.870
which of course are made out of metal.

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:41.780
The Tom Scott bot is
head and shoulders only.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:44.500
So we got his head, we got a neck,

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:46.270
but there's no body underneath.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:50.659
Once we created the robot,
we'd scan that robot again,

00:03:50.659 --> 00:03:54.443
and using that scan we know exactly
the range of motions of the robot.

00:03:54.443 --> 00:03:56.930
We then do a video of the person,

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:00.600
or get some reference
videos of the person,

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:05.520
and try and match their
little here-and-there

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:06.860
with the mouth and the eyes.

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:08.280
Everyone's different the way

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:11.790
they actually express themselves,
we try and match those.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:12.623
- Hello?

00:04:12.623 --> 00:04:14.260
- Hi Tom, how are you doing?
- How are you?

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:15.250
- Yeah, good thanks.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.083
- A bit nervous!

00:04:16.083 --> 00:04:17.221
- Are you ready for this?

00:04:17.221 --> 00:04:18.180
[laughs]

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:19.857
- I honestly, I don't know
how I feel about this.

00:04:19.857 --> 00:04:21.233
I really, I really don't.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.090
You said it was through
there on the right.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:27.300
- It's through here,
so go ahead, you first.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.133
- Okay.

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:34.161
Oh, that is weird.

00:04:34.161 --> 00:04:36.711
[laughter]

00:04:36.711 --> 00:04:40.243
That is really, wow.

00:04:41.528 --> 00:04:43.611
[laughs]

00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:46.550
Do you want to take that camera?

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:50.000
That is, oh wow.

